## 🔍 What Happened
Characters:
1. Sister Isolde Carrow - A fallen cleric of St. Aldric's order who lost her faith after a failure. She carries a silver holy symbol she no longer believes in. Her divine magic still works, which terrifies her. She has a dry wit, is a compulsive caretaker, and is terrified of being responsible for another death. Relevant traits: Medicine +5, Religion +4, Insight +4. Advantage on saves vs. undead fear effects. Disadvantage on Wisdom saves when she witnesses suffering.

2. Dorian Ashgrove - A disgraced investigator who was dismissed for pursuing a case no one wanted solved. He carries a leather notebook with obsessive detail and drinks too much gin. He is brilliant, paranoid, occasionally right about things no one else notices. Relevant traits: Investigation +6, Perception +4, History +4. Has a network of informants (1d4 days to get a reply). Disadvantage on social checks when sober.

3. Emmett Grave - A self-proclaimed archaeologist who knows twelve ways to open a locked crypt and has used all of them. He is surprisingly knowledgeable about burial customs, ward-markings, and funerary rites. He has no moral qualms and is extremely cheerful about death. Relevant traits: Sleight of Hand +5, Thieves' Tools +5, Arcana +3, History +3. Can identify undead type on a DC 12 Arcana check. Immune to the Frightened condition (he's seen worse).

4. Vera Nighthollow - A witch who lives on the edge of law and forest. She knows herbalism, folk magic, and things her grandmother told her never to repeat. She speaks to animals (they don't like what they tell her). She is distrusted by respectable people but sought out when they are desperate. Relevant traits: Nature +5, Arcana +5, Animal Handling +4, Deception +4. Prepared: Detect Magic at will. Can brew potions (1 hour, herbalism kit, DC 12 Arcana). Disadvantage on checks in cities, advantage in wilderness/ruins.

